{
  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "G_apply",
      "kind": "goal",
      "description": "Commands delivered to the FSM through normal Apply processing should have the committed replication support promised by the library.",
      "source_ids": [
        "docs/apply.md:1:100",
        "api.go:800:907",
        "fsm.go:1:180",
        "commitment.go:1:104"
      ],
      "scope": {
        "description": "Normal command replication, commitment and FSM delivery, with membership and leadership context preserved.",
        "assumptions": [],
        "excluded": [
          "A guarantee that an unsuccessful Apply future means the command cannot subsequently take effect.",
          "Automatic preservation of command effects across explicit user snapshot restoration."
        ],
        "parameters": []
      },
      "pending": [
        "Read the leader commitment consumer and processLogs before checking end-to-end FSM delivery.",
        "Establish storage guarantees, request construction and membership transition prerequisites."
      ],
      "grounding": {
        "behavior_ids": [
          "api.go:800:907",
          "commitment.go:1:104",
          "fsm.go:1:180"
        ],
        "expectation_ids": [
          "docs/apply.md:1:100",
          "fsm.go:1:180"
        ],
        "binding_ids": [
          "B_commit_recalculate"
        ],
        "derivation": "The Apply documentation promises commitment before FSM application, and the FSM interface describes application after majority commitment. The commitment implementation calculates a supporting index, but the intervening delivery path is not supplied. This establishes audit significance without establishing end-to-end correctness.",
        "applicability": "Applies to ordinary replicated commands. API comments explicitly preserve uncertainty after leadership loss and describe abortion by user restore.",
        "unresolved": [
          "How commitment notifications, log delivery and future completion correlate to the same command.",
          "Which membership configuration governs each advancement.",
          "Whether reported replication evidence satisfies the documented durable-storage expectation."
        ],
        "conflicts": [],
        "alternatives": [
          "BatchingFSM provides a distinct delivery mechanism that must retain the applicable commitment guarantee.",
          "Apply timeout controls admission and is not a general completion deadline; BatchApplyCh further weakens the documented start-time guarantee."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "O_commit",
      "kind": "obligation",
      "description": "Within a commitment instance, each commit-index advancement must be monotonic, supported by a strict majority of the current tracked voters at that index, and reach the instance's first index of the leader term.",
      "source_ids": [
        "commitment.go:1:104",
        "docs/apply.md:1:100",
        "configuration.go:150:310"
      ],
      "scope": {
        "description": "The commitment accumulator under constructor, match and configuration-update operations. Support is evaluated when an advancement occurs, not retroactively against later voter sets.",
        "assumptions": [
          "Input reports represent their claimed server, leadership instance and matching log prefix; this is an unverified boundary, not a checked guarantee.",
          "The caller supplies the correct first index of the leader term and an admissible membership history."
        ],
        "excluded": [
          "Durability of supplied match reports.",
          "Safety of arbitrary membership histories.",
          "FSM application and client future completion."
        ],
        "parameters": []
      },
      "pending": [
        "Verify constructor and configuration-update callers.",
        "Resolve local and remote match-report producers before interpreting a local result as evidence about real commitment."
      ],
      "grounding": {
        "behavior_ids": [
          "commitment.go:1:104"
        ],
        "expectation_ids": [
          "commitment.go:1:104",
          "docs/apply.md:1:100"
        ],
        "binding_ids": [
          "B_commit_new",
          "B_commit_configuration",
          "B_commit_match",
          "B_commit_recalculate"
        ],
        "derivation": "The structure comments define match indexes as stored matching prefixes, commitIndex as monotonically increasing quorum support, and startIndex as a prerequisite for marking entries committed. The implementation filters voters, retains existing voter reports, and calculates an order statistic. The candidate checks that this consumer implements its stated accumulator responsibility.",
        "applicability": "Immediately applicable as a bounded accumulator check. Its contribution to G_apply remains conditional on report provenance, membership admission and correct initialization.",
        "unresolved": [
          "Actual ordering of membership changes relative to log append and commitment.",
          "Initialization of startIndex at leadership establishment.",
          "Lifetime of delayed reports across membership or address changes."
        ],
        "conflicts": [],
        "alternatives": [
          "Preserving a previously committed index after a membership change does not itself require a fresh quorum in the new voter set; the proposed check concerns new advancements."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "O_append_evidence",
      "kind": "obligation",
      "description": "A successful AppendEntries response used to advance a follower's match index must establish the matching stored prefix claimed by that advancement, in the relevant peer and request context.",
      "source_ids": [
        "raft.go:1440:1584",
        "replication.go:202:298",
        "commitment.go:1:104",
        "docs/apply.md:1:100",
        "transport.go:1:83"
      ],
      "scope": {
        "description": "Follower acceptance and the non-pipelined replication response handoff into matching-prefix evidence.",
        "assumptions": [],
        "excluded": [
          "Byzantine peers or forged transport responses.",
          "A requirement that followers finish FSM application before acknowledging replication."
        ],
        "parameters": []
      },
      "pending": [
        "Read updateLastAppended and request construction.",
        "Read the LogStore contract and identify applicable implementations.",
        "Inspect the pipeline and snapshot alternatives before generalizing beyond replicateTo."
      ],
      "grounding": {
        "behavior_ids": [
          "raft.go:1440:1584",
          "replication.go:202:298",
          "commitment.go:1:104"
        ],
        "expectation_ids": [
          "commitment.go:1:104",
          "docs/apply.md:1:100"
        ],
        "binding_ids": [
          "B_append_entries",
          "B_replicate_to"
        ],
        "derivation": "commitment.match documents its input as agreement of stored log prefixes. replicateTo passes a successful request to updateLastAppended after its response-term check. appendEntries validates the predecessor, handles conflicting entries and stores new entries before setting Success. These producers and consumers support a candidate obligation, but neither the report-construction helper nor the storage contract is established here.",
        "applicability": "Applicable to successful non-pipelined AppendEntries responses used as match evidence. Durability and request identity remain unresolved rather than assumed proved.",
        "unresolved": [
          "The exact index reported for empty requests.",
          "Request-to-response and peer-identity correlation.",
          "Storage semantics after successful deletion followed by failed append.",
          "Whether cached last-log metadata can influence later acceptance after a storage failure.",
          "Producer-side constraints on entry continuity and same-term entry identity."
        ],
        "conflicts": [],
        "alternatives": [
          "Snapshot installation and pipelined replication can produce progress through different paths.",
          "The documented follower FSM lag permits an acknowledgment before application; application completion is not required by this obligation."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "G_recovery",
      "kind": "goal",
      "description": "Snapshot creation and log reclamation should preserve a usable recovery representation of the captured FSM state and its associated Raft metadata.",
      "source_ids": [
        "README.md:1:100",
        "fsm.go:1:180",
        "snapshot.go:125:215",
        "api.go:631:708",
        "config.go:138:245"
      ],
      "scope": {
        "description": "Automatic snapshots, their metadata, compaction and startup recovery.",
        "assumptions": [],
        "excluded": [
          "Treating NoSnapshotRestoreOnStart as a promise that Raft restores application state.",
          "Equating explicit user restoration with automatic recovery."
        ],
        "parameters": []
      },
      "pending": [
        "Read snapshot-store publication and closure contracts.",
        "Read compaction and FSM snapshot-capture logic.",
        "Establish application recovery responsibility when startup FSM restoration is disabled."
      ],
      "grounding": {
        "behavior_ids": [
          "snapshot.go:125:215",
          "api.go:631:708"
        ],
        "expectation_ids": [
          "README.md:1:100",
          "fsm.go:1:180",
          "config.go:138:245"
        ],
        "binding_ids": [
          "B_take_snapshot"
        ],
        "derivation": "The README describes snapshot restoration as substituting for replay of old logs. takeSnapshot persists captured state and metadata before initiating compaction, while startup recovery adopts snapshot indexes and configuration. Their dependency creates a recovery obligation spanning both sides of reclamation.",
        "applicability": "The ordinary recovery path restores the FSM. With NoSnapshotRestoreOnStart, metadata is still adopted but application recovery is explicitly delegated to another mechanism.",
        "unresolved": [
          "Crash durability and discoverability of a successfully closed snapshot.",
          "Correct association between captured FSM state and returned index and term.",
          "Actual log ranges removed by compaction.",
          "Failure behavior when recovery tries multiple snapshots."
        ],
        "conflicts": [],
        "alternatives": [
          "Application-owned startup recovery is explicitly supported by NoSnapshotRestoreOnStart."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "O_snapshot_publish",
      "kind": "obligation",
      "description": "takeSnapshot must associate the captured index and term with a committed configuration no newer than that index, and must not publish last-snapshot metadata or invoke log compaction before persistence and sink closure report success.",
      "source_ids": [
        "snapshot.go:125:215",
        "fsm.go:1:180",
        "api.go:631:708"
      ],
      "scope": {
        "description": "Local snapshot orchestration, including capture errors, configuration-index rejection, persistence errors and closure errors.",
        "assumptions": [
          "The FSM snapshot future describes the state actually captured.",
          "A successful sink closure has the meaning promised by the snapshot-store contract, which remains unread."
        ],
        "excluded": [
          "Proving physical crash durability from a successful callback.",
          "Proving the compaction deletion range.",
          "Proving arbitrary client FSM snapshot implementations correct."
        ],
        "parameters": []
      },
      "pending": [
        "Resolve the sink closure contract, including the interface expectation that Persist itself closes the sink.",
        "Verify snapshot-future metadata production."
      ],
      "grounding": {
        "behavior_ids": [
          "snapshot.go:125:215",
          "api.go:631:708"
        ],
        "expectation_ids": [
          "fsm.go:1:180",
          "README.md:1:100",
          "snapshot.go:125:215"
        ],
        "binding_ids": [
          "B_take_snapshot"
        ],
        "derivation": "Recovery adopts snapshot indexes and configuration, and compaction removes replay material. Therefore publication and reclamation depend on successful creation of the corresponding recovery representation. takeSnapshot's explicit index guard and persistence ordering provide the bounded implementation path to check.",
        "applicability": "Applicable to takeSnapshot orchestration. This obligation checks ordering and association, while the stronger meaning of successful persistence remains an external contract boundary.",
        "unresolved": [
          "Whether repeated Close calls are supported by every applicable sink.",
          "Whether the configuration and captured index describe a legally reachable common history.",
          "Whether errors after snapshot publication leave a usable snapshot despite an unsuccessful API result."
        ],
        "conflicts": [
          "FSMSnapshot.Persist is documented to close the sink, and takeSnapshot also closes it. This is an unresolved interface-composition question, not a demonstrated defect."
        ],
        "alternatives": [
          "A sink may intentionally support idempotent closure.",
          "A captured snapshot may be discarded before persistence; the FSM interface explicitly permits this and requires Release."
        ]
      }
    }
  ],
